{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793784180,"nonce":"jni7tpXTZdryJOI6WwBUqLgElVQ=","message":"Partial progress on per-frame root overhead: replace the boxed FrameView with a stack value borrowed by FrameRoots. The borrow keeps the descriptor address stable until the guard unregisters it. Build the view only when roots are wanted. Normal return, error, unwind, and explicit OSR release retain the existing deregistration behavior. No change to the collector root registry, span liveness filtering, or the bounded frame pool.\n\nThe new interpreter_roots integration test reads both active frame descriptors through the host heap span interface inside a nested call. It verifies live scalar payloads and removal of all frame roots after normal return, an error, and panic unwinding. The host-heap bridge still allocates its separate SpanRoot wrapper; this change removes the interpreter descriptor allocation.\n\nNUC allocation diagnostics use separate instrumented release builds, a warmed fib(12) invocation (465 interpreted frames), one measured invocation per arm. Baseline at 68b4de44: roots off 2 allocations/75 requested bytes; roots on 471 allocations/16267 bytes. Direct stack descriptors: roots off 2/75, roots on 6/1387. The difference is exactly 465 allocations and 14880 requested bytes, one 32-byte descriptor per frame. Registry allocations/locking remain follow-up work; this broader issue stays open.\n\nInitial uncooled timing results are not accepted as performance evidence: they immediately followed builds, and the user reported sustained heat. Raw measurements are retained under v1. Final timings must follow all builds and allocation diagnostics, precede final conformance, and use frozen ordinary release binaries. The runner records per-sample temperature, idle activity, and throttle counters, requires package \u003c=60 C and maximum core \u003c=65 C before samples; the final idle gate allows \u003c=0.75 busy CPU cores globally and \u003c=0.5 on logical CPUs 0-7 (the pinned P cores and their SMT siblings), and checks for Cargo/rustc before and after samples. Initial cooldown is at least 60 seconds and requires 20 consecutive seconds satisfying those conditions.\n\nThe initial all-machine idle limit (0.5 cores) paused excessively for desktop activity outside the selected physical cores. A direct /proc/stat sample showed 0.47-0.58 busy cores globally versus 0.22-0.35 on the selected physical cores plus their siblings. The refined guard keeps the same bound on those cores and explicitly records both activity measures. Already-collected samples under the stricter global bound necessarily satisfy both final bounds and were retained. A separate earlier cold batch was aborted when an unrelated Rayde Cargo build started; those data are archived separately under interrupted-cold and do not contribute to the accepted comparison. The Zyntax checkout was restored and verified; subsequent timings use frozen executables and copied benchmark fixtures.\n\nThe cooled stack-descriptor microbenchmark repeats still regress call-heavy execution: recursive roots-on 181.93 -\u003e 187.98 ns (about 3.3% slower); 24-site roots-on 173.84 -\u003e 184.70 ns (about 6.2% slower). Scalar control 37.26 -\u003e 36.53 ns. The stack-descriptor version is not suitable to land as a speed optimization despite removing 465 allocations. Next experiment: reuse owned boxed root descriptors in the existing bounded frame pool, preserving their current access pattern and ownership.\n\nCompleted ordinary release before/after comparison: all 17 Lua benchmark outputs and all 22 supported Python kernel checksums agree with their reference interpreters. pidigits remains explicitly unsupported. Seven alternating whole-process rounds per kernel, Python uses the harness fast scaling (five times each kernel scaling, minimum one iteration). LLVM enabled; default native library snapshots. Lua geometric-mean baseline/candidate wall ratio 0.99683; Python 1.00065 excluding unsupported pidigits and thermally unstable nqueens. Whole-process wall time is effectively unchanged.\n\nThermal evidence: 614 recorded process samples including partial-case repeats; all starts at \u003c= 60.0 C package. Workloads themselves reached 93.0 C. One nqueens candidate sample incremented thermal-throttle counters; the interrupted timing was not retained, and that kernel remains marked thermally unstable despite the later complete retry avoiding throttling. No clean speed conclusion for nqueens. Builds and conformance did not overlap the accepted comparison.\n\nValidation for the rejected stack version: 25 focused compiler/GC/OSR tests and Clippy passed. The preceding stack version also passed 942 local TypeScript checks, 40 Lua checks, and the full official corpus (5948/5948 files, 1151 PASS, 12 existing FAIL, 2234 UNSUPPORTED, 3428 SKIP, no regressions). No reviewed baseline changed. No stack-descriptor change was committed; it is being replaced by a separate bounded boxed-descriptor reuse experiment.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-frame-roots/v2-nuc/ and /private/tmp/zyntax-frame-roots/zyntax-frame-roots-v2-results.tar.gz.","files":null}]}